Riku Saikkonen writes:

>Some of my own [ideas for 3.2]:
>
>Better talking (the chat command is quite unimaginative...).
>Commands like:
># say Hey, wizard! Hand the Amulet over or else...
># shout Aargh! ("You hear a shout.", "Someone shouts 'Aargh!'", ...)
>or something simpler:
># threaten Nymph
># plead to Dragon

Well, nice idea, but it doesn't seem to me that they'd achieve
anything.  They'd only be useful for the next idea...

>A multi-player option (on computers where multitasking is supported).
>The #say and #shout commands would of course have their uses; maybe a
>#whisper too. Hmm... Perhaps a flag in compilation; MUD_MODE or
>something.

Many people have examined this idea, and given it up.  I'm afraid it
requires way too much redesign, and loses a lot of the philosophy
of Nethack.  For instance, at the moment Nethack is NOT real-time.
Between keystrokes, you can think, stretch, get a Coke, take a shower,
visit your Aunt, or take off to Mimas for seven years of uranium
mining, and NO TIME ELAPSES IN THE GAME.  How could anyone *hope* to
implement this sort of effect in a MU Nethack?  Would *you* be willing
to wait for someone else's keystrokes before your character moved?

The alternative is to go realtime, and I don't think that's fair,
either.  A fast typist's character could so easily wipe out a slower
typist's character that it wouldn't make anyone laugh.

>Bargaining in shops.

Nice idea, but I'm afraid I don't like it.  (Sorry!  I am being negative
this morning, aren't I?)  Two reasons.

1.  Bargaining already occurs (but without you, the player, being aware of it).
    This is why high charisma characters can buy things more cheaply than low
    charisma characters.

2.  Check out Moria or Angband.  Both have haggling, and in the more recent
    versions, you can turn haggling off.  (That is, make it silent, so that
    you, the player, don't have to personally haggle with the shopkeeper.)
    This is because haggling can get very very very boring, especially when
    you're bickering (once again!) over the cost of food.

>A surrender option (#surrender, surrender to the monsters around one -
>maybe a jail too?).

Nice idea, but isn't that just shift-Q?

>A better move command; something that would work rather like teleport
>(with tp control) but would only work in places already seen and with a
>direct walking route. (Move destination? (arrow keys, . to keep))

This is a darn good idea.

Of course, you'd want to "wake up" when a monster gets too close, with a
"Carry on with the walk" command, for if you decide the monster's not
worth worrying about.

># finger l8.this.game
>Monsters on level 8: 1 * red dragon, 12 * orc ...
># finger red_dragon@l8.this.game
>Red Dragon; hitpoints 78(80); inventory: a ruby wand, a potion of
>sickness ... :-)

I presume you mean only for the levels you'd already seen.  Yes, I like this,
too.

On the whole, I always like the ideas which mean that I, the player, don't
need to keep scraps of paper and pens near my computer.  Nethack is effectively
DMed by a computer, so should (at least in theory) be good at doing things
computers are good at (like keeping track of boring details and following
predefined routes).

And FINALLY, my reply to Eddy Simons' request:

>ok, quick question for all you hack Guru's.  What is the hardest class to
>ascend with?  A Tourist?  A Barbarian?  A Mage with a 3 int?  Gimme your
>opinions.

Tourists aren't too difficult.  With all that food, they can afford to
  take their time, and gain XP levels at low Dungeon levels.
Elves are not easy to start, but once they've started they're not too
  difficult.  All those intrinsics, you know.
Knights should be not-difficult, but they keep dying on me.

I'd point to Healers and Rogues as the hardest classes (Rogues particularly
due to the Quest bug, Healers because of the lack of weapon/armour at the
start).

